The '''2018 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League]], the 47th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 9th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at the [[Parc Olympique Lyonnais]] in [[Décines-Charpieu]], [[Lyon Metropolis|Lyon]], France on 16 May 2018,<ref name="host">{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0252-0cd2199fd757-085c4991f75c-1000--lyon-to-host-2018-uefa-europa-league-final/ |title=Lyon to host 2018 UEFA Europa League final |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 December 2016 |access-date=9 December 2016}}</ref> between [[French Football Federation|French]] side [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] and [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spanish]] side [[Atlético Madrid]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0245-0e98c298b3d9-e3234be6af4d-1000--all-you-need-to-know-uefa-europa-league-final/|title=All you need to know: UEFA Europa League final|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=3 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0245-0e98c2988cd1-9002b1530af6-1000--marseille-v-atletico-meet-the-europa-league-finalists/|title=Marseille v Atlético: Meet the Europa League finalists|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=3 May 2018}}</ref>

Atlético Madrid won the match 3–0 for their third Europa League title.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0245-0e98ccf1cec2-0418a5d239cc-1000--griezmann-inspires-atletico-to-europa-league-glory/ |title=Griezmann inspires Atlético to Europa League glory |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 May 2018 |access-date=18 May 2018}}</ref> As winners, Atlético Madrid earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]], [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], in the [[2018 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified to enter the group stage of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d81c2c9caf3-d5f66f92b4db-1000--evolution-of-uefa-club-competitions-from-2018/|title=Evolution of UEFA club competitions from 2018|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=26 August 2016}}</ref> but since they already qualified through their league performance, the berth reserved was given to the third-placed team of the [[2017–18 Ligue 1]], [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]], as Ligue 1 was the 5th-ranked association according to next season's access list.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0248-0e98d6a7579b-b83f689f894f-1000--who-is-in-the-champions-league-group-stage/|title=Who is in the 2018/19 Champions League group stage?|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=26 May 2018}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:OL-Angers Groupama Stadium 11.jpg|thumb|275px|left|The [[Parc Olympique Lyonnais]] was renamed to "Stade de Lyon" for the match]]
The [[Parc Olympique Lyonnais]] was announced as the final venue on 9 December 2016, following the decision of the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Nyon]], Switzerland.<ref name="host"/>

==Background==
[[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] reached their third final after a 3–2 aggregate win against [[Austrian Football Bundesliga|Austrian]] side [[FC Red Bull Salzburg|Red Bull Salzburg]], clinched after extra time. They lost the final in both previous occasions ([[1999 UEFA Cup Final|1999]] and [[2004 UEFA Cup Final|2004]]). This was also their fifth final in all seasonal UEFA competitions, having also played in two [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup/Champions League]] finals (winning in [[1993 UEFA Champions League Final|1993]]; and losing in [[1991 European Cup Final|1991]]).

[[Atlético Madrid]] also reached their third final after a 2–1 aggregate win against [[Premier League|English]] side [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]. They won the final in both previous occasions ([[2010 UEFA Europa League Final|2010]] and [[2012 UEFA Europa League Final|2012]]). This was also their ninth final in UEFA seasonal competitions, having played in three [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup/Champions League]] finals (losing in [[1974 European Cup Final|1974]], [[2014 UEFA Champions League Final|2014]] and [[2016 UEFA Champions League Final|2016]]) and three [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] final (winning in [[1962 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1962]]; and losing in [[1963 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1963]] and [[1986 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1986]]).

The two teams had played each other twice in the [[UEFA Champions League|Champions League]]. Atlético Madrid won the first match, while the second was drawn in the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League]] group stage.

